# Controller env for the SOW-005 reconcile + gate when run locally. Copy to .env and fill in.
# .env is GITIGNORED; .env.example is tracked. `npm run reconcile` loads .env automatically
# (node --env-file-if-exists=.env). In CI these are GitHub Actions secrets, not a file.
# The Worker has its own local env at workers/signup/.dev.vars (see .dev.vars.example there).

# Stripe restricted CONTROLLER key: Customers + Subscriptions READ only (the reconcile never writes Stripe).
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY="rk_..._controller_read_only"
GITHUB_CONTENT_REPO="gbti-network/gbti.network"
# Fine-grained gbtilabs PAT (contents + pull-requests + statuses on the content repo).
GITHUB_BOT_TOKEN="..."
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN="..."
DISCORD_GUILD_ID="000000000000000000"
DISCORD_MEMBER_ROLE_ID="000000000000000000"
DISCORD_TRIAL_ROLE_ID="000000000000000000"
# The Locked role: reconcile assigns it to lapsed/banned accounts to lock them out (via the role's
# channel overwrites) without kicking them from the guild.
DISCORD_LOCKED_ROLE_ID="000000000000000000"
RESEND_API_KEY="..."
RESEND_FROM="noreply@gbti.network"
# BOT_GITHUB_ID intentionally unset: automation runs as the gbtilabs superadmin (exempt by role).

# sow-314: the Shop Talk calendar credential (Google). The Worker adds paid and trial members to the owner's
# recurring Saturday event as guests, which is what admits them to the Meet without knocking.
#
# NAMED GOOGLE_CALENDAR_* RATHER THAN GOOGLE_*, and that is the load-bearing part. A second Google client is
# coming for member sign-in, and the whole security argument for splitting them is that the sign-in client
# must never be able to reach the owner's calendar. Two clients sharing a GOOGLE_ prefix are one careless
# edit away from being mistaken for each other, so the future one is GOOGLE_SIGNIN_OAUTH_*.
#
# Scope is `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events` and nothing else: read and write on EVENTS only,
# with no access to the calendar list, calendar settings, or any other Google data.
#
# The refresh token survives only because the consent screen is "In production". A project left in "Testing"
# expires refresh tokens after SEVEN DAYS, and the sweep would then fail silently about a week after shipping.
# Verified in production 2026-09-07; re-check that before blaming the code.
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_CLIENT_ID="000000000000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com"
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_CLIENT_SECRET="GOCSPX-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_REFRESH_TOKEN="1//xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
